The repo does not compile under Xcode 26.5 at this HEAD for THREE pre-existing, out-of-scope reasons — none
of which my changes introduce or can fix within scope:
  R1  Duplicate filename `ScheduledExportsPanel.swift` exists in BOTH apps/apple/TeslaSync/feature-views/ and
      apps/apple/TeslaSync/Features/System/ (both git-tracked, committed before this task). Swift 6 errors:
      "Filename used twice ... used to distinguish private declarations". (driver-level abort)
  R2  Toolchain `#Preview` macro ambiguity: under Xcode 26.5 the SwiftUI `#Preview` macro and the
      implicitly-linked UIKit/AppKit `#Preview` macro both resolve, so EVERY file using `#Preview` errors
      ("ambiguous use of 'Preview'") — 149 pre-existing files incl. core Sources/Components/UI/{Badges,Cards,
      Buttons,Disclosure,Typography}.swift (which `import SwiftUI` only, exactly like a clean page would).
      This is an environment/toolchain incompatibility, repo-wide, predating this task.
  R3  Parallel-salvage symbol collisions in apps/apple/TeslaSync/{shared-surfaces,feature-views,...}: hundreds
      of "ambiguous use of 'sm'/'md'/'lg'/'opacity'/'init'" and "'TirePressureUnit' is ambiguous" errors from
      duplicate type definitions in those salvaged dirs (documented by the sibling DriveScore/SmartCharge logs).

Because the compiler cancels remaining work after the alphabetically-early A* salvage batches fail, the build
aborts before emitting any diagnostics for my D-named files — i.e. a green compile is unattainable for ANY
code in this environment, including the unmodified pre-existing repo. To keep MY deliverable provably clean of
the R2 toolchain issue, the page ships WITHOUT `#Preview` blocks (previews are dev-only, not a parity item);
the three data states are instead exercised by the model unit tests (Sample/Empty/Failing data sources).

